"# What happens internally when you call `spark-submit`?\n\n`spark-submit` is the command-line utility used to launch a Spark application on a cluster.\n\nInternally, it processes application configurations, identifies the Cluster Manager and deployment mode, launches the Driver, arranges resource allocation, starts Executors, and enables distributed task execution.\n\nThe exact sequence depends on whether Spark runs in Client Mode or Cluster Mode, and whether the Cluster Manager is YARN, Kubernetes, or Standalone.\n\n## 1. Example Spark Submit Command\n\n```\nspark-submit \\\n  --master yarn \\\n  --deploy-mode cluster \\\n  --executor-memory 8G \\\n  --executor-cores 4 \\\n  --num-executors 5 \\\n  --driver-memory 4G \\\n  --conf spark.sql.shuffle.partitions=200 \\\n  job.py\n```\n\n| Parameter                          | Meaning                                                                    |\n| ---------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--master yarn`                    | Use YARN as the Cluster Manager                                            |\n| `--deploy-mode cluster`            | Run the Driver inside the cluster                                          |\n| `--executor-memory 8G`             | Allocate 8 GB JVM heap per Executor                                        |\n| `--executor-cores 4`               | Allocate 4 CPU cores per Executor                                          |\n| `--num-executors 5`                | Request 5 Executors initially, subject to configuration and availability   |\n| `--driver-memory 4G`               | Allocate 4 GB JVM heap to the Driver                                       |\n| `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions=200` | Configure the initial shuffle partition count for supported SQL operations |\n| `job.py`                           | PySpark application to execute                                             |\n\n## 2. Internal Execution Flow\n\n[spark-reference:submit]\n\nThis is the conceptual flow. In actual Spark implementations, some initialization and resource-allocation activities overlap or occur in a different order depending on the Cluster Manager.\n\n## 3. Step-by-step Internal Explanation\n\n### Step 1: Spark submission process starts\n\nWhen we execute `spark-submit`:\n\n1. The shell script initializes the Spark submission environment.\n2. It launches Spark's submission logic, implemented through the `org.apache.spark.deploy.SparkSubmit` class.\n3. Spark parses command-line arguments and loads relevant configurations.\n4. It determines the Cluster Manager, deployment mode, application entry point, and requested resources.\n\nSpark also prepares the dependencies and runtime settings needed to launch the application.\n\n### Step 2: Application resources are prepared\n\nSpark determines which application resources must be made available to the Driver and Executors.\n\nDepending on the deployment environment, these may include:\n\n- Application JAR or Python files.\n- Additional dependencies specified using `--jars` or `--py-files`.\n- Configuration files.\n- Other resources supplied using `--files` or `--archives`.\n\nFor example, with YARN Cluster Mode, Spark may stage application resources in distributed storage so cluster containers can access them.\n\n### Step 3: Driver is launched\n\nThe Driver's location depends on the deployment mode.\n\nClient Mode:\n\nThe Driver runs on the machine executing `spark-submit`.\n\nCluster Mode:\n\nThe Driver is launched inside the cluster.\n\nFor example, in YARN Cluster Mode, YARN launches an ApplicationMaster container, and the Spark Driver runs within that ApplicationMaster process.\n\nIn YARN Client Mode, the ApplicationMaster instead primarily coordinates resource requests while the Driver runs on the submitting machine.\n\n### Step 4: SparkContext is initialized\n\nWhen application code creates a SparkSession, Spark initializes or obtains its SparkContext.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nspark = (\n    SparkSession.builder\n    .appName(\"SalesProcessing\")\n    .getOrCreate()\n)\n```\n\nThe SparkContext establishes Spark's execution environment.\n\nImportant components initialized as part of the Driver's execution environment include:\n\n- DAG Scheduler\n- Task Scheduler\n- Scheduler Backend\n- Block Manager and related coordination services\n- RPC communication infrastructure\n\nThe Driver also establishes communication with the appropriate Cluster Manager.\n\n### Step 5: Resources are allocated\n\nSpark requests computing resources for Executors through the Cluster Manager.\n\nFor the example configuration:\n\n```\nNumber of Executors = 5\nCPU cores per Executor = 4\nExecutor heap memory = 8 GB\n```\n\nThe requested total is:\n\n- 20 Executor CPU cores\n- 40 GB Executor JVM heap memory\n\nAdditional memory overhead is required beyond the configured JVM heap.\n\nOn YARN, the ResourceManager allocates containers according to the cluster's resource availability and scheduling policies.\n\nThe NodeManagers then launch and manage the allocated containers.\n\nImportant: The Cluster Manager allocates resources; it does not schedule individual Spark tasks.\n\n### Step 6: Executors are launched\n\nAfter resources become available:\n\n1. Executor processes are launched on worker nodes.\n2. Executors initialize their runtime environments.\n3. Executors establish communication with the Driver.\n4. Executors register with the Driver.\n5. Executors become available to execute tasks.\n\nEach Executor can run multiple tasks concurrently, depending on its allocated CPU cores and task resource requirements.\n\n### Step 7: Spark executes jobs\n\nThe application's transformations build execution plans.\n\nWhen an action such as `count()`, `collect()`, or `write` is invoked, Spark triggers computation.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"s3://bucket/sales/\")\nresult = df.filter(\"amount > 1000\")\nresult.groupBy(\"region\").count().show()\n```\n\nThe execution proceeds conceptually as follows:\n\n1. Spark creates and optimizes the query execution plan.\n2. An action triggers one or more Spark jobs.\n3. The DAG Scheduler divides jobs into stages based on dependencies, including shuffle boundaries.\n4. The Task Scheduler schedules stage tasks on available Executors.\n5. Executors process their assigned data partitions.\n6. Executors exchange shuffle data when necessary.\n7. Executors report task completion or failures to the Driver.\n\nThe Driver coordinates subsequent stages until the computation completes.\n\n### Step 8: Application completes and resources are released\n\nAfter the application finishes:\n\n- Output is written or the requested results are returned.\n- Spark reports application status.\n- The SparkContext is stopped during normal application shutdown.\n- Executors are terminated and their resources released.\n- The Cluster Manager cleans up application resources according to its implementation.\n- Execution logs and metrics may remain available through the Spark History Server or cluster logging systems.\n\nSpark applications can also remain active, such as long-running streaming jobs, until they are explicitly stopped or fail.\n\n## 4. How does `spark-submit` differ across Cluster Managers?\n\n| Cluster Manager | What happens during submission?                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| YARN            | Submits an application to YARN. The ApplicationMaster coordinates resource allocation, and Executors run in YARN containers. In Cluster Mode, the Driver runs inside the ApplicationMaster process. |\n| Kubernetes      | Spark submits a Driver Pod. The Driver requests Executor Pods through the Kubernetes API.                                                                                                           |\n| Standalone      | Spark communicates with the Standalone Master, which coordinates launching Drivers (Cluster Mode) and Executors on worker nodes.                                                                    |\n\n## 5. Interview-ready answer (60–90 seconds)\n\n> When we execute `spark-submit`, Spark first initializes the submission process, parses the application configurations, and identifies the Cluster Manager and deployment mode.\n>\n> Depending on the deployment mode, the Driver is launched either on the submitting machine in Client Mode or inside the cluster in Cluster Mode.\n>\n> Once the application initializes SparkContext, Spark establishes its execution environment and requests Executor resources through the Cluster Manager.\n>\n> The Cluster Manager allocates CPU and memory resources, after which Executors are launched on worker nodes and registered with the Driver.\n>\n> When the application triggers an action, Spark creates an optimized execution plan and divides the computation into jobs, stages, and tasks.\n>\n> The Driver schedules tasks across available Executors, which process data partitions in parallel and report execution status back to the Driver.\n>\n> Once the application completes, Spark releases the allocated resources and reports the final application status.\n\nKey takeaway: `spark-submit` is responsible for application submission and startup, while the Driver is responsible for coordinating distributed execution and Executors perform the actual computation."
